//? Title: Chudnovsky Pi: Fast Exact-Series Convergence and a Floating Plateau
//? Subtitle: Stable recurrence, actual additions, geometric truncation bounds and underflow
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Computing more digits of pi requires both a rapidly converging formula and
arithmetic precise enough to retain its corrections. The Chudnovsky series
has an asymptotic gain of about 14.18 decimal digits per term, but a double
result cannot keep gaining digits at that rate. We inspect three actual native
additions, retain the original one-to-four-term experiment with properly labelled
reference gaps, derive an exact-arithmetic truncation bound, and follow the
floating recurrence through stagnation and term underflow. A stable canonical
recurrence replaces factorial evaluation that could overflow before cancellation.
)

\toc

> Three terms: a nonzero correction that the sum cannot retain

Write A=13591409, B=545140134 and C=640320. With

$$
a_k=\frac{(-1)^k(6k)!(A+Bk)}{(3k)!(k!)^3C^{3k}},\qquad
K=426880\sqrt{10005},\qquad \pi=\frac{K}{\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}a_k},
$$
\alttext(The coefficient term is the signed factorial ratio times A plus B times k, divided by C to the power three k. Pi is the constant K divided by the infinite sum.)

the first term is A and the second is approximately −2.553837355·10⁻⁷.
That second correction changes the rounded sum. The third term is nonzero,
but is too small to change the double accumulator. The following table and
diagram use records captured during one native evaluation. An unchanged
floating sum is an arithmetic observation, not exact equality to the infinite
series or a guarantee of correctly rounded pi.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/chudnovsky_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::chudnovsky_pi_trace(3);
  if(t.steps.size()!=3 || t.first_unchanged_index!=2 || t.steps[2].term==0)return 1;
  std::print("| k | actual term | sum before | sum after | sum changed | rounded pi estimate |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: |\n");
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(60,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle node;node.width=5.9;node.height=3.2;node.font_size=20;
  for(const auto& s:t.steps) {
    std::print("| {} | {:.17e} | {:.17e} | {:.17e} | {} | {:.17g} |\n",s.index,s.term,s.sum_before,s.sum_after,s.sum_changed,s.estimate);
    if(!s.sum_changed)node.fill={254,240,138};
    const auto id=std::format("term{}",s.index);
    diagram.node(id,{8.0*s.index,0},std::format("k={}\nterm {:+.3e}\nsum changed: {}\npi ~ {:.17g}",s.index,s.term,s.sum_changed,s.estimate),node);
    if(s.index)diagram.edge(std::format("term{}",s.index-1),id,"next term");
  }
  diagram.text({8,3.1},"First three actual Chudnovsky additions",22)
      .text({8,-3.1},"The third term is nonzero; its rounded addition leaves the sum unchanged",19)
      .save_svg("build/chudnovsky_method.svg");
  std::print("\nRequested {}, evaluated {}, ratio evaluations {}, sum additions {}.\n",t.terms_requested,t.terms_evaluated,t.ratio_evaluations,t.sum_additions);
}
```
