\alttext(Three boxes show actual terms at indices zero, one and two. The first two additions change the sum; the third box is gold because its nonzero correction leaves the rounded sum unchanged.)

> Deriving a recurrence that avoids factorial overflow

The familiar reciprocal form is

$$
\frac1\pi=12\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}
\frac{(-1)^k(6k)!(545140134k+13591409)}{(3k)!(k!)^3\,640320^{3k+3/2}}.
$$
\alttext(The Chudnovsky reciprocal-pi series multiplies a signed factorial ratio and a linear coefficient, divided by a large power of 640320.)

Taking C^(3/2)/12 outside the sum gives K above, since C=64·10005.
Forming every factorial and power separately is unnecessary and can overflow
even when the final term is tiny. Cancelling consecutive factorial factors gives

$$
\frac{a_{k+1}}{a_k}=
-\frac{24(6k+1)(2k+1)(6k+5)}{(k+1)^3C^3}
 \frac{A+B(k+1)}{A+Bk}.
$$
\alttext(The next coefficient is the current coefficient times a negative rational factor from the cancelled factorials and the ratio of successive linear coefficients.)

The native kernel starts at a₀=A, multiplies by this ratio, adds each term and
returns K/S. It evaluates the three polynomial factors as bounded fractions,
using n=k+1: (6−5/n)(2−1/n)(6−1/n). Converting the index before arithmetic
also avoids overflowing expressions such as 6k at large int indices.
The scalar and traced interfaces share this executing kernel; the trace does
not reconstruct a second explanatory computation.

The old routine's call signature remains. Its factorial/power path is replaced,
so the last output bits can change. Nonpositive term counts now throw
std::invalid_argument instead of dividing an empty sum by zero. No arbitrary
precision or binary splitting is performed by this evaluator.

> The original four-term experiment, with honest reference gaps

The original report compared a double estimate with a double pi literal and
called their difference the error to pi. That measures agreement between two
rounded numbers. A zero difference is not proof of zero real error. We retain
all four requested term counts and print both that original comparison and a
gap to std::numbers::pi_v<long double>, explicitly identifying both as rounded
references. The latter has more significand bits on this machine; on a platform
where long double has no extra precision, it may offer no finer comparison.

No artificial 10⁻¹⁷ floor is inserted into the plot. A zero reference gap is
reported as equality to that reference and omitted from logarithmic points.
The plot therefore shows measured floating differences, not fourteen new
correct digits per step.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/chudnovsky_pi.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <numbers>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  constexpr double original_reference=3.14159265358979323846;
  constexpr auto extended_reference=std::numbers::pi_v<long double>;
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::chudnovsky_pi_trace(4);
  std::vector<double> count,log_gap;int equal_reference=0;
  std::print("Double significand bits: {}. Long-double reference significand bits: {}.\n\n",std::numeric_limits<double>::digits,std::numeric_limits<long double>::digits);
  std::print("| terms | estimate | gap to original double literal | gap to rounded extended pi |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(const auto& s:t.steps) {
    const auto double_gap=std::fabs(s.estimate-original_reference);
    const auto extended_gap=std::fabs(static_cast<long double>(s.estimate)-extended_reference);
    if(extended_gap>0){count.push_back(s.index+1);log_gap.push_back(static_cast<double>(std::log10(extended_gap)));}else ++equal_reference;
    std::print("| {} | {:.17g} | {:.9e} | {:.9e} |\n",s.index+1,s.estimate,double_gap,extended_gap);
  }
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.line(count,log_gap,"reference gap",{37,99,235},1.5).points(count,log_gap,"reference gap",{37,99,235},5)
      .legend({"rounded-reference gap"},{{37,99,235}})
      .title("Double estimates reach a rounding plateau")
      .x_label("number of requested terms").y_label("log10 gap to rounded extended pi")
      .x_limits(0.7,4.3).y_limits(-17,-12).save_svg("build/chudnovsky_pi.svg");
  std::print("\nZero extended-reference gaps omitted from logarithmic points: {}.\n",equal_reference);
}
```
