// result_end
\caption(The original one-to-four-term inputs show a floating plateau; both reference columns are labelled as rounded-number comparisons.)
\alttext(A logarithmic gap falls between one and two terms and then stays flat. Zero gaps, if any, are omitted rather than replaced by an invented error floor.)

Even the first correction cannot display its full asymptotic gain here: the
one-term approximation is already close to pi, leaving only a few further
observable digits before double precision saturates. The exact series and the
floating implementation answer different accuracy questions.

> Why the asymptotic gain is about 14.18 digits

The factorial ratio contributes a factor that the old explanation omitted.
Taking the limit in the recurrence gives

$$
\rho=\lim_{k\to\infty}\left|\frac{a_{k+1}}{a_k}\right|
=\frac{24\cdot6\cdot2\cdot6}{640320^3}
=\frac{1728}{640320^3}
=\frac1{151931373056000}.
$$
\alttext(The limiting term ratio magnitude is 1728 divided by 640320 cubed, equal to one over 151931373056000.)

Thus −log10(ρ)≈14.1816474627. The factor is not merely 1/C³, which would
predict an incorrect rate. The convergence is geometric in term count, often
called linear convergence, with a very small ratio. It is not an assertion
that each finite-precision call gains exactly fourteen correct digits.

For k≥1 the ratio magnitude is at most ρ. One direct check multiplies through
by positive denominators. Writing x=k−1, the required difference is

$$
72(k+1)^3(A+Bk)-(6k+1)(2k+1)(6k+5)(A+B(k+1))
=66835011381+143166737776x+94141694952x^2+19625044824x^3>0.
$$
\alttext(The difference proving the uniform ratio bound is a polynomial in k minus one with four positive integer coefficients.)

The initial ratio is larger than ρ but still much less than one. All terms
alternate and decrease in magnitude. Set b₁=|a₁|=120(A+B)/C³ and L=A−b₁>0.
Every exact partial sum S_N and the infinite sum S are at least L. The
alternating-series remainder and the ratio bound give, for N≥1,

$$
|S_N-S|\le |a_N|\le b_1\rho^{N-1},\qquad
\left|\frac K{S_N}-\pi\right|
=\frac{K|S_N-S|}{S_NS}
\le E_N=\frac{Kb_1\rho^{N-1}}{L^2}.
$$
\alttext(The omitted alternating tail is bounded by the first omitted term, itself bounded geometrically from the first correction. Dividing by the positive partial and infinite sums gives an upper bound on exact-series pi truncation error.)

The native log-bound helper evaluates log10(E_N) directly. It is a rounded
evaluation of this mathematical bound, not an outward-rounded interval and not
a bound on normaliser, recurrence, addition or division rounding. Its line can
continue far below floating error without implying that the returned double
has gained those digits.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/chudnovsky_truncation.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<double> count,log_bound;
  std::print("| mathematical terms N | log10 theoretical truncation bound |\n| ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(int n=1;n<=12;++n) {
    const auto bound=dm::algorithms::chudnovsky_log10_truncation_bound(n);
    count.push_back(n);log_bound.push_back(bound);std::print("| {} | {:.9f} |\n",n,bound);
  }
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.line(count,log_bound,"theory",{234,88,12},1.5).points(count,log_bound,"theory",{234,88,12},4)
      .legend({"exact-series bound"},{{234,88,12}})
      .title("Exact-series truncation bound in the log domain")
      .x_label("mathematical term count N").y_label("log10 theoretical bound E_N")
      .y_limits(-180,-5).save_svg("build/chudnovsky_truncation.svg");
  std::print("\nAsymptotic theoretical decimal gain: {:.12f}.\n",dm::algorithms::chudnovsky_digits_per_term());
  const auto deep=dm::algorithms::chudnovsky_log10_truncation_bound(100);
  std::print("100-term log bound remains finite: {}; converting its magnitude to double underflows: {}.\n",std::isfinite(deep),std::pow(10.0,deep)==0);
}
```
