\caption(Actual recorded counts and the final terms separate unchanged sums from term underflow, even for an INT_MAX request.)

The recurrence uses O(m) floating operations for m actual additions through
underflow. The ordinary call uses O(1) auxiliary storage; the trace uses O(m)
owned storage and performs prefix divisions for display. This is not the bit
complexity of a multiple-precision implementation. Binary splitting and large
integer multiplication are useful for high-precision work, but are not hidden
behind this double API. Bellard's 2010 implementation paper describes that
separate high-precision workflow.

> Validation and independent evidence

The following boundary checks exercise public input contracts and reproduce
the scalar/trace agreement for a count that previously overflowed factorials.
Calls have no shared mutable state; copied records remain independent.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  int rejected=0;
  for(const auto n:{0,-1,std::numeric_limits<int>::min()}) {
    try{(void)dm::algorithms::chudnovsky_pi(n);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){++rejected;}
    try{(void)dm::algorithms::chudnovsky_pi_trace(n);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){++rejected;}
    try{(void)dm::algorithms::chudnovsky_log10_truncation_bound(n);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){++rejected;}
  }
  bool negative_ratio=false;try{(void)dm::algorithms::chudnovsky_term_ratio(-1);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){negative_ratio=true;}
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::chudnovsky_pi_trace(29);auto copy=t;copy.steps[0].term=0;
  if(rejected!=9 || !negative_ratio || !std::isfinite(t.value) || t.steps[0].term!=13591409)return 1;
  std::print("Nonpositive count rejections: {} of 9. Negative ratio index rejected: {}.\n",rejected,negative_ratio);
  std::print("29-term request now finite: {}; actual additions {}, scalar/trace agreement {}.\n",std::isfinite(t.value),t.sum_additions,t.value==dm::algorithms::chudnovsky_pi(29));
  std::print("Copied record mutation leaves original first term {}.\n",t.steps[0].term);
  const auto log_bound=dm::algorithms::chudnovsky_log10_truncation_bound(std::numeric_limits<int>::max());
  std::print("INT_MAX mathematical log bound remains finite: {}; it is not an error certificate for the double result.\n",std::isfinite(log_bound));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Nonpositive count rejections: 9 of 9. Negative ratio index rejected: true.
29-term request now finite: true; actual additions 25, scalar/trace agreement true.
Copied record mutation leaves original first term 13591409.
INT_MAX mathematical log bound remains finite: true; it is not an error certificate for the double result.
// result_end
\caption(Invalid counts reject explicitly; large valid requests and owned record copies have defined behavior.)

Ten native tests compare the first twelve coefficients with independent
long-double factorial expressions, verify 1,000 full-range random ratio indices
and the positive polynomial identity, and check geometric bounds against one
hundred first-omitted terms. Counts 1–4096, exact underflow boundaries, INT_MAX
requests, signs, invalid counts and record ownership pass. All ten cases also
pass AddressSanitizer and UBSan; existing pi, hexadecimal-digit and CORDIC
regressions remain in the native runner.

A separate development oracle uses Python's standard Decimal arithmetic at
240 decimal digits. It computes pi from Machin's arctangent identity, rather
than this series, checks the native term/prefix values through underflow and
seven ratios, and compares twelve finite Chudnovsky truncations with the
geometric bound. Values below that oracle's own decimal resolution are marked
as such. This is validation evidence; report computation stays in native C++.
There is no natural observational dataset for these numerical identities.

> What the calculation establishes

The factorial cancellation gives a stable recurrence and the correct limiting
ratio, including its factor 1728. Exact-series truncation improves rapidly,
while the double accumulator reaches a plateau and eventually loses the terms
themselves to underflow. Actual native records and separately labelled
mathematical bounds make those three facts visible without inventing precision.

> References

- D. V. Chudnovsky and G. V. Chudnovsky (1989), *The computation of classical
  constants*, Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 86(21), 8178–8182.
  https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.86.21.8178
- Fabrice Bellard (2010), *Computation of 2700 billion decimal digits of Pi using
  a Desktop Computer*, fourth revision, section 2.
  https://bellard.org/pi/pi2700e9/pipcrecord.pdf
  The present evaluator uses the cancelled term recurrence in double precision;
  the geometric truncation bound is derived above.
